**Verdict.**

- **KNOWN — literature audit:** The best general rigorous bound I could verify for prime-field DL remains \(L_p[1/2,\sqrt2+o(1)]\). I found no published improvement applying to every prime, or to a density-one set of primes, either unconditionally or assuming GRH. This is a literature-search conclusion, not a theorem excluding improvements.
- **PROVED below:** Theorem A’s square-class post-processing does **not** transfer verbatim to arbitrary prime divisors of \(p-1\). However, there is another route: use **exact multiplicative relations**, via Ge’s polynomial-time relation-lattice algorithm. This avoids Schirokauer maps, class-group computations, factoring \(p-1\), and large-order power-residue characters.
- **PROVED, conditional on the internal relation-supply claims:** This alternative gives a uniform prime-field DL algorithm with constant
  \[
  c_C(\theta)=\min_{a>0}\max\left\{
       a+\frac1{\sqrt2},\;
       a+\frac{\theta}{2a},\;
       Ca
  \right\},
  \tag{1}
  \]
  where \(C\ge2\) is an absolute polynomial exponent for exact multiplicative-relation post-processing. Consequently, sufficiently large **fixed** degrees give \(c<1\), and, for every fixed \(\varepsilon>0\), give \(c<1/\sqrt2+\varepsilon\).
- **Not established here:** The advertised degree-nine DL constants \(0.99720187\ldots\) and infimum \(0.91923882\ldots\). They require a sufficiently small quantified exponent \(C\), or a faster specialized relation algorithm. “Polynomial time” alone does not establish them.

The positive result is therefore a **proved reduction from the internal supply theorem**, not an independent certification of that theorem. I treat the supplied notes and audit as unrefereed.

I use
\[
L_x[\alpha,c]=\exp\!\bigl((c+o(1))(\log x)^\alpha(\log\log x)^{1-\alpha}\bigr).
\]
Below, **PROVED** identifies deductions proved here; their internal assumptions are stated explicitly. **KNOWN** identifies external results. No repository files were changed.

---

**1. Literature**

There are two distinctions that substantially affect this comparison: whether the ambient group order is supplied, and whether the result concerns arbitrary targets in the full group or only a subgroup.

**1(a). Prime fields**

**KNOWN.** Pomerance, *Fast, rigorous factorization and discrete logarithm algorithms* (1987), pp. 119–143, establishes the randomized rigorous bound
\[
L_p[1/2,\sqrt2+o(1)]
\]
for DL in \(\mathbb F_p^\times\). His own later account describes the rigorous smoothness-testing ingredient and gives the same constant. [Pomerance 1987](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/disclog.pdf), [Pomerance 2008, §6, pp. 393–395](https://library.slmath.org/books/Book44/files/11carl.pdf).

**KNOWN — search result.** I found no general rigorous prime-field improvement on \(\sqrt2\), including under GRH, and no density-one-primes improvement. Special hypotheses on \(p-1\), or a small subgroup order, can of course make Pohlig–Hellman much faster; those are different assertions.

**HEURISTIC.** The usual general NFS bound
\[
L_p[1/3,(64/9)^{1/3}+o(1)]
\]
is not a GRH-only theorem. GRH does not supply the needed joint smoothness and relation-generation assertions. Schirokauer explicitly formulates this complexity as conjectural. [Schirokauer 2008, §2.8](https://library.slmath.org/books/Book44/files/12oliver.pdf).

**1(b). Extension fields**

| Status | Result and hypotheses | Rigorous bound |
|---|---|---|
| **KNOWN** | Pomerance’s polynomial index calculus, fixed characteristic | \(L_{p^n}[1/2,\sqrt2+o(1)]\) |
| **KNOWN** | Lovorn Bender–Pomerance, *Rigorous discrete logarithm computations in finite fields via smooth polynomials* (1998), \(p\le n^{o(n)}\) | \(L_{p^n}[1/2,\sqrt2+o(1)]\) |
| **KNOWN** | Kleinjung–Wesolowski, Theorem 1.1; arbitrary prime \(p\), arbitrary \(n\ge1\) | \((pn)^{\,2\log_2 n+O(1)}\) |
| **KNOWN** | Consequently, fixed \(p\) | \(\exp((2/\log2+o(1))(\log n)^2)\) |

The Lovorn Bender–Pomerance paper also gives subexponential algorithms whenever \(n\to\infty\), arbitrarily slowly; this does **not** mean the displayed \(\sqrt2\) bound holds throughout that larger range. Pomerance makes that distinction in his account. [Original paper, 1998](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper115.pdf), [Pomerance 2008, p. 395](https://library.slmath.org/books/Book44/files/11carl.pdf).

Kleinjung–Wesolowski first appeared in 2019, was published electronically in 2021, and appeared in *JAMS* **35** (2022), 581–624. Its theorem applies to **all** finite fields of the stated size; an unproved special-presentation hypothesis is not retained. [Theorem 1.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1906.10668), [published article](https://doi.org/10.1090/jams/985).

**KNOWN.** Earlier Granger–Kleinjung–Zumbrägel results proved quasi-polynomial complexity for explicit infinite families admitting the required presentation. They should not be substituted for KW’s all-fields theorem. Lido’s Theorem 1.1 (2022 preprint) supplies another rigorous quasi-polynomial result for small characteristic, without a sharper explicit leading constant in its statement. [GKZ, 2018](https://doi.org/10.1090/TRAN/7027), [Lido, Theorem 1.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2206.10327).

**PROVED — interpreting KW in \(L\)-notation.** Put \(Q=p^n\), \(t=\log Q\). Its logarithmic running time is bounded by
\[
\frac{2}{\log2}\log n\,(\log p+\log n)+O(\log p+\log n).
\]
Thus, for fixed \(0<\alpha<1\), if
\[
\log p=(\kappa+o(1))t^\alpha(\log t)^{-\alpha},
\]
KW gives
\[
L_Q\!\left[\alpha,\frac{2\kappa(1-\alpha)}{\log2}+o(1)\right].
\tag{2}
\]
In particular, this gives genuine fixed-constant \(L[1/3]\) and \(L[1/2]\) bounds in the corresponding characteristic ranges.

One must not replace that hypothesis by \(p=L_Q[\alpha,\kappa]\) while keeping the same constant: that introduces an additional \(\log\log Q\) factor into the exponent.

**KNOWN — limitation of the verified record.** I found no general rigorous NFS \(L[1/3,c]\) theorem for the usual medium/large-characteristic regimes merely under GRH. For fixed extension degree \(n>1\), KW itself gives no subexponential-in-\(Q\) conclusion. Pomerance credits Lovorn Bender with a rigorous subexponential result for \(n=2\); the commonly quoted constant is \(3/2\), but I could not verify the original numbered theorem or its final publication and therefore do not certify that numerical entry. [Pomerance’s statement, p. 395](https://library.slmath.org/books/Book44/files/11carl.pdf).

**1(c). Imaginary quadratic class groups**

Write \(\Delta<0\) for the discriminant.

| Status | Task and assumptions | Bound |
|---|---|---|
| **PROVED — generic comparison** | DL, unconditionally, arbitrary \(\Delta\) | \(|\Delta|^{1/4+o(1)}\), using BSGS and the standard class-number upper bound |
| **KNOWN** | Hafner–McCurley (1989), class-group structure, ERH/GRH | \(L_{|\Delta|}[1/2,\sqrt2+o(1)]\) |
| **KNOWN** | Vollmer (2000), quadratic-field DL, GRH | \(L_{|\Delta|}[1/2,3\sqrt2/4+o(1)]\) |
| **KNOWN** | Enge–Gaudry (2002), Theorem 1: cyclic class group of **known order**, ERH | \(L_{|\Delta|}[1/2,1+o(1)]\) |
| **KNOWN** | Biasse–Erukulangara, main theorem, class-group computation, GRH | \(L_{|\Delta|}[1/2,3/\sqrt8+o(1)]\) |

Here \(3/\sqrt8=3\sqrt2/4\). Biasse–Erukulangara appeared online in 2021 and in *Advances in Mathematics of Communications* **17** (2023), 1388–1405. Their theorem improves the rigorously established Hafner–McCurley **structure-computation** constant. It must not be confused with the known-order DL setting. [Hafner–McCurley 1989](https://research.ibm.com/publications/a-rigorous-subexponential-algorithm-for-computation-of-class-groups), [Vollmer 2000](https://doi.org/10.1007/10722028_39), [Enge–Gaudry, Theorem 1](https://www.impan.pl/shop/publication/transaction/download/product/83152), [Biasse–Erukulangara, main theorem](https://www.aimsciences.org/article/doi/10.3934/amc.2021055).

**KNOWN.** The recent de Boer–Pellet-Mary–Wesolowski preprint, Theorem 1.3, gives ERH-rigorous class-group and compact-unit computation for arbitrary number fields, with its stated discriminant, degree, and zeta-residue dependencies. It explicitly leaves finite-field DL applications uninvestigated. It supplies no published improvement to the prime-field benchmark above. [2025 preprint, Theorem 1.3 and p. 5](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.01588).

**1(d). Hyperelliptic Jacobians**

Here \(L\) is measured against \(q^g\), not against \(q\).

**KNOWN.** Enge, *Computing discrete logarithms in high-genus hyperelliptic Jacobians in provably subexponential time* (2002), Theorem 1.1: for fixed \(\vartheta>0\), instances satisfying \(g\ge\vartheta\log q\) have unconditional expected complexity
\[
L_{q^g}\!\left[
 \frac12,\;
 \frac5{\sqrt6}
 \left(\sqrt{1+\frac3{2\vartheta}}
       +\sqrt{\frac3{2\vartheta}}\right)+o(1)
\right].
\tag{3}
\]
This holds over arbitrary finite base fields. [Enge 2002, Theorem 1.1](https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-01-01363-1).

**KNOWN.** Enge–Gaudry (2002), Theorem 1, gives
\[
L_{q^g}[1/2,\sqrt2+o(1)]
\]
when the Jacobian is cyclic of known order and \(g/\log q\to\infty\). Their §6, Example 5, gives, for \(g\ge\vartheta\log q\),
\[
L_{q^g}\!\left[
 \frac12,\;
 \sqrt2\left(\sqrt{1+\frac1{2\vartheta}}
             +\sqrt{\frac1{2\vartheta}}\right)+o(1)
\right].
\tag{4}
\]
Their §7 specifies the additional information or complement sampling needed for noncyclic groups and subgroups. [Enge–Gaudry 2002](https://www.impan.pl/shop/publication/transaction/download/product/83152).

**KNOWN.** Heß’s general-curve work supplies unconditional \(L_{q^g}[1/2,O(1)]\) algorithms beyond the hyperelliptic setting. I could not verify an optimal numerical constant from the draft. An exact ingredient is its **Theorem 13**, reproduced as Theorem 1 in Enge–Gaudry–Thomé:
\[
\frac{\psi(\nu,\mu)}{q^\nu}
\ge
\exp\!\left[-u\log u
 \left(1+\frac{\log\log u+3/(1-\epsilon)}{\log u}\right)\right],
\]
where \(u=\nu/\mu\),
\(3\log_q(14g+4)\le\mu\le\nu^\epsilon\), and
\(u\ge2\log(g+1)\), for sufficiently large parameters uniformly in \(q,g\). [Exact restatement and hypotheses](https://members.loria.fr/EThome/files/l13.pdf).

**KNOWN.** For fixed genus, Diem’s *On the discrete logarithm problem in class groups of curves* (2011), Theorem 1, proves expected
\[
\widetilde O(q^{\,2-2/g})
\]
for fixed \(g\ge2\), with the paper’s input representation. This is exponential in \(\log(q^g)\) at fixed genus, and is a different asymptotic regime. [Diem, Theorem 1](https://www.math.uni-leipzig.de/~diem/preprints/small-genus.pdf).

**HEURISTIC.** Enge–Gaudry–Thomé’s \(L[1/3]\) algorithms concern suitable low-degree plane-curve families and retain explicit smoothness and relation-lattice heuristics. They are not rigorous \(L[1/3]\) algorithms for general hyperelliptic Jacobians. [Heuristics 5 and 6](https://members.loria.fr/EThome/files/l13.pdf).

**Citation limitation.** I verified the numbered statements identified above. The accessible Pomerance-1987 and Bender–Pomerance scans did not yield searchable theorem text; I have not invented theorem numbers for them or for Vollmer’s conference paper.

---

**2. What transfers from Theorem A**

To avoid confusing special-\(q\) primes with group-order primes, write \(q_{\rm sp}\in J\) for a descent prime and \(\ell\mid p-1\) for a group-order prime.

**PROVED — transfer conditional on the internal estimates.**

| Roadmap step | Transfer to \(N=p\) |
|---|---|
| 1: polynomial construction and identities | Transfers. \(F(g_0,g_1)=pw\) gives the required reduction map to \(\mathbb F_p\). |
| 2: special-\(q\) enumeration and batch smoothness | Transfers, with the same verified certificates and costs. |
| 3: FM | Its stated hypotheses concern the polynomial family, sizes and coprimality. Primality of \(p\) causes no new obstruction. The internal analytic proof remains an assumption. |
| 4: LD* | Same observation: the multiplier average, Kern and L2_fam calculations do not require compositeness. |
| 5: Paley–Zygmund | Transfers unchanged once the two moments are granted. |
| 6: field rejection | Transfers and simplifies: proper-factor exits disappear. Content, irreducibility and derivative rejections remain. |
| 7: target splitting and field selection | Needs the change from uniformly random squares to uniformly random elements of \(\mathbb F_p^\times\). The counting becomes easier. |

The factoring preprocessing and the hypothesis \(\omega(N)\ge2\) are discarded.

**PROVED — full-group version of S/S′.** Let \(R\) be uniform on \(\mathbb F_p^\times\). For a product set \(S\) in a window below \(\sqrt{p/2}\), put
\[
n(R)=\#\{(u,v)\in S^2:u\equiv Rv\pmod p\}.
\]
Now
\[
\sum_R n(R)=|S|^2
\]
exactly: there is no square-class restriction.

The determinant argument in S still turns congruent ratios into equal rational ratios. The bounded-\(k\) multiplicity argument of S′ still gives
\[
\sum_Rn(R)^2\le c_k|S|^2.
\]
Consequently,
\[
\Pr[n(R)>0]\ge \frac{|S|^2}{c_k(p-1)}.
\tag{5}
\]
The primitive shortest vector is accepted using the same divisor-closed “at most \(k\) distinct primes” rule. Thus K’s selection argument retains its \(L_p[1/2,o(1)]\) losses.

**PROVED — producing uniform targets.** Initially suppose \(g\) generates \(\mathbb F_p^\times\), put \(n=p-1\), and write \(h=g^x\). Choose independent uniform \(a,b\in\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z\) and set
\[
R=g^ah^b.
\]
Then \((a,b)\mapsto(a+xb,b)\) is a bijection. Therefore:

1. \(R\) is uniform;
2. conditional on \(R\), \(b\) remains uniform;
3. this remains true after any acceptance test depending only on \(R\), the fields and independent coins.

Keep \(a,b\) outside the descent transcript. This conditional uniformity replaces DD.

**PROVED — two independently randomized fields.** Two sides do not require an unproved independence assertion about their good-prime sets. If
\[
s_q=\Pr_A[q\in G_A],\qquad \mathbb E_qs_q=\gamma,
\]
then for independent fields \(A_+,A_-\),
\[
\mathbb E_{A_+,A_-}
 \frac{|G_{A_+}\cap G_{A_-}|}{|J|}
 =\mathbb E_qs_q^2\ge\gamma^2.
\tag{6}
\]
Thus \(\gamma\ge L^{-o(1)}\) gives the same kind of supply for pairs of fields. Requiring all split primes to lie in the intersection proves a sufficient lower bound, even if numerator primes are ultimately descended only on the positive side and denominator primes only on the negative side.

**Internal supply assumption RS.** The remaining construction assumes the uniform conclusions of the internal FM, LD*, PZ and field-rejection arguments, with the corrections in `thmA_proof.md`. They imply that \(m=B L^{o(1)}\) accepted targets can be collected in expected time
\[
L_p\!\left[
 \frac12,\;
 \max\left\{a+\frac1{\sqrt2},\;
            a+\frac{\theta}{2a},\;
            2a\right\}+o(1)
\right].
\tag{RS}
\]
The \(2a\) term safely includes the \(B\)-part of each batch smoothness search when \(B>S^2\).

---

**3. Why the direct \(\ell\)-th-power transplant fails**

**PROVED — the obstruction is real.** For a number field \(K\) and a finite set \(S\) of prime ideals, define
\[
H_{S,\ell}
=
\{z\in K^\times:v_{\mathfrak p}(z)\equiv0\pmod\ell
                  \text{ for }\mathfrak p\notin S\}/K^{\times\ell}.
\]
There is an exact sequence
\[
0\longrightarrow
\mathcal O_{K,S}^{\times}/\mathcal O_{K,S}^{\times\ell}
\longrightarrow H_{S,\ell}
\longrightarrow
\operatorname{Cl}(\mathcal O_{K,S})[\ell]
\longrightarrow0.
\tag{7}
\]
Indeed, write \((z)=\mathfrak a^\ell\) away from \(S\); the class of \(\mathfrak a\) is \(\ell\)-torsion, and its kernel consists precisely of \(S\)-units modulo powers.

Hence cancelling ideal valuations modulo \(\ell\) does not establish that the element is an \(\ell\)-th power.

The dimension estimate *does* generalize:
\[
\dim_{\mathbb F_\ell}H_{S,\ell}
\le |S|+r_1+r_2-1+\dim\mu(K)/\mu(K)^\ell
       +\frac{\log h_K}{\log\ell}.
\tag{8}
\]
At fixed degree and the notes’ heights this is \(O_d(\log p)\). **A dimension bound is not an algorithm for computing separating maps.**

**KNOWN algebra; unproved uniform applicability.** Standard virtual logarithms work under conditions such as:

- \(\ell\) is coprime to the relevant class number;
- the chosen Schirokauer maps have full rank on units modulo \(\ell\)-th powers;
- bad primes and the chosen order are handled correctly.

Schirokauer maps are efficiently evaluable in their usual good-prime setting. Their required rank on units is not automatic: generalized Wieferich phenomena can make the first-order maps vanish on nontrivial unit classes. GRH does not itself prove the necessary rank condition for every sampled field. [Schirokauer’s construction and discussion](https://library.slmath.org/books/Book44/files/12oliver.pdf).

**PROVED — why lv6’s analysis is not uniform in \(\ell\).** For square classes, the relevant extensions have degree \(2d\). For general power classes one encounters
\[
K(\zeta_\ell,z^{1/\ell}),
\]
whose degree can grow on the order of \(d\ell(\ell-1)\). The fixed-degree Chebotarev argument, its discriminant estimates and its computational costs cannot simply be reused with constants independent of \(\ell\).

Moreover, computing a power-residue symbol as an element of \(\mu_\ell\) is not automatically computing its coordinate in \(\mathbb F_\ell\); the latter may itself require an order-\(\ell\) DL.

For **fixed** \(\ell\), Kummer extensions and character computations are finite-degree problems, and a separate rigorous adaptation is plausible and accessible. That does not establish the needed uniform bound for \(\ell\) as large as \(p/2\).

**PROVED — a second cost problem.** A dependency with coefficients \(0,\ldots,\ell-1\) can produce an expanded algebraic product of bit length proportional to \(\ell B\log p\). Lv6’s square-root routine does not establish a polynomial-in-\(\log\ell\) algorithm for manipulating or extracting roots of such products. A factored representation helps storage; it does not by itself prove the required root-extraction bound.

**PROVED — prime powers need attention.** Knowing a logarithm modulo \(\ell\) in the full group means knowing it in the quotient
\[
\mathbb F_p^\times/(\mathbb F_p^\times)^\ell.
\]
It does not automatically give every Pohlig–Hellman digit for \(\ell^e\). Subsequent residual targets can be trivial in that quotient. One needs compatible \(\ell^e\)-information, a justified lifting method, or a different algorithm.

Small \(\ell\) can be handled by generic Pohlig–Hellman digit computations in \(\widetilde O(\sqrt\ell)\) group operations. This does not settle large repeated prime factors.

**PROVED — factoring \(p-1\) can bind.** Factoring \(p-1\) with the published Lenstra–Pomerance algorithm costs \(L_p[1/2,1+o(1)]\). Adding that as preprocessing already prevents a claimed total constant below \(1\), unless the factorization is supplied or another factoring bound is invoked. The alternative below avoids that cost.

---

**4. Avoiding the obstruction with exact relations**

This is the main constructive result.

**KNOWN — Ge’s theorem.** Given an explicitly represented number field \(K\) and a finite list \(z_1,\ldots,z_m\in K^\times\), the lattice
\[
\{c\in\mathbb Z^m:\prod_i z_i^{c_i}=1\}
\]
has a basis computable in polynomial time in the **total input length**. The polynomial is uniform in the field input, not merely a fixed-degree statement with an uncontrolled degree-dependent exponent.

The source is Ge’s 1993 Berkeley thesis. The theorem is explicitly restated by Lenstra on slide 15 of his 2026 lectures; Derksen–Jeandel–Koiran also identify the polynomial-time result in §3.2 of their 2005 paper. The full relation-lattice result should not be confused with Ge’s published equality-testing theorem alone. [Lenstra’s exact statement](https://swc-math.github.io/aws/2026/2026LenstraLecture2Slides.pdf), [Derksen–Jeandel–Koiran, §3.2](https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=90c27dcf269ae44839a4d0c95e22e71ca28d4445&repid=rep1&type=pdf).

**PROVED — bounded-support lifts of accepted targets.**

For one field, the supplied row identity is
\[
R_i=q_i t^{-E_i}\psi\!\left(\prod_Pz_P^{e_{iP}}\right).
\]
Define the actual algebraic element
\[
\xi_i=q_i t^{-E_i}\prod_Pz_P^{e_{iP}}\in K^\times.
\tag{9}
\]
Then \(\psi(\xi_i)=R_i\).

For two fields, distribute the numerator and denominator factors between them to obtain
\[
R_i=\psi_+(\xi_{i,+})\psi_-(\xi_{i,-}).
\tag{10}
\]
For example, absorb the rational factor \(q_i\) into \(\xi_{i,+}\).

Each element has an explicit power-basis representation of \(O_{d,k}(\log p)\) bits. There are only \(2k\) special-\(q\) atoms per row.

Its prime-ideal support lies above rational primes at most \(B\), together with primes dividing the fixed field quantities \(t_\pm g_{1,\pm}\). This follows from
\[
N(z_P)=t^{d-1}F(P),
\]
the smoothness certificates, and the rational row formula. In particular, **the special-\(q\) primes disappear from the algebraic norms**.

Consequently there is a common support set \(S_\pm\) with
\[
|S_\pm|\le d\pi(B)+O_d(\log p).
\tag{11}
\]
No factorization of those fixed field quantities is needed to use this upper bound.

Reduction is legitimate on the localization generated by these elements: their denominators have nonzero images modulo \(p\), as certified by the row construction.

**PROVED — existence of an exact dependence.**

By the \(S\)-unit theorem, the subgroup generated by the pairs
\[
(\xi_{i,+},\xi_{i,-})\in K_+^\times\times K_-^\times
\]
has torsion-free rank at most
\[
r_++r_-+|S_+|+|S_-|
\le 2d\pi(B)+O_d(\log p).
\tag{12}
\]
Collect more rows than this bound.

Apply Ge’s algorithm in each field and intersect the two integer relation lattices using polynomial-time integer linear algebra. This yields a nonzero integer vector \(v\) satisfying
\[
\prod_i\xi_{i,+}^{v_i}=1,\qquad
\prod_i\xi_{i,-}^{v_i}=1.
\]

Set
\[
r=\gcd_i v_i,\qquad c_i=v_i/r.
\]
The vector \(c\) is primitive. Each product
\[
\prod_i\xi_{i,\pm}^{c_i}
\]
is a root of unity. An odd-degree number field has only the roots of unity \(\{\pm1\}\), since every other cyclotomic degree is even. Thus
\[
\prod_i R_i^{c_i}=(-1)^\delta
\tag{13}
\]
for a computable \(\delta\in\{0,1\}\).

The sign can be determined by modular exponentiation. There is no need to expand enormous algebraic power products.

This proves existence and computability of the needed dependence without:

- computing a class group or unit group;
- assuming a Schirokauer-map rank condition;
- computing \(\ell\)-th power characters;
- assuming that collected rows span a prescribed relation lattice.

**PROVED — extraction and its success probability.**

First suppose \(g\) is a generator, \(n=p-1\), and \(h=g^x\). Put
\[
A=\sum_i c_i a_i\pmod n,\qquad
D=\sum_i c_i b_i\pmod n.
\]
Equation (13) implies
\[
A+xD\equiv\delta n/2\pmod n.
\tag{14}
\]
If \(\gcd(D,n)=1\), return
\[
x=(\delta n/2-A)D^{-1}\pmod n.
\tag{15}
\]

Conditional on the complete descent certificates, the \(b_i\) are independent uniform residues. Because \(\gcd_i c_i=1\), the map
\[
(b_i)\longmapsto\sum_i c_i b_i
\]
is surjective modulo \(n\). Hence \(D\) is exactly uniform modulo \(n\), and
\[
\Pr[\gcd(D,n)=1]=\frac{\varphi(n)}n.
\tag{16}
\]
Even the elementary bound \(\varphi(n)/n\ge1/(1+\log_2n)\) suffices: order the distinct prime factors \(p_j\), use \(p_j\ge j+1\), and telescope
\(\prod_j(1-1/p_j)\ge1/(r+1)\).

Fresh batches therefore give only a polylogarithmic expected overhead. This handles all prime powers of \(p-1\) simultaneously, using gcds and inversion modulo the unfactored integer \(p-1\).

**PROVED — removing the supplied-generator assumption.**

For arbitrary \(g,h\) with the promise \(h\in\langle g\rangle\):

1. sample a random auxiliary base \(t\);
2. try to compute verified logarithms \(t^u=g\), \(t^v=h\);
3. solve \(ux\equiv v\pmod{p-1}\), and verify \(g^x=h\).

A random \(t\) is a generator with inverse-polylogarithmic probability. To avoid getting stuck on a nongenerator, use time limits: at each doubled budget, try \(O(\log p)\) independent auxiliary bases. Once the budget exceeds a constant multiple of the expected generator-case time, Markov’s inequality gives constant success probability for each generator trial. The failure probability per stage can be made below \(1/4\), giving finite expected total cost of the same \(L\)-exponent.

All outputs are verified. No generator certification or factorization of \(p-1\) is required.

---

**5. Cost and exact constants**

**PROVED, assuming RS and Ge’s theorem.** The input to exact-relation post-processing has length
\[
T=B(\log p)^{O_{d,k}(1)}.
\]
Fix implementations of Ge’s algorithm, lattice intersection, coefficient processing and modular extraction. Their combined cost is at most
\[
B^C(\log p)^{O_{d,k}(1)}
\]
for some **absolute** \(C\ge2\).

Therefore the total constant is exactly the expression in (1). The \(Ca\) term cannot be silently replaced by \(2a\).

For \(0<\theta<1\), minimizing gives
\[
\boxed{
c_C(\theta)=
\begin{cases}
(1+\theta)/\sqrt2,
    &(C-1)\theta\le1,\\[2mm]
C\sqrt{\theta/(2(C-1))},
    &(C-1)\theta>1.
\end{cases}}
\tag{17}
\]
In the first case take \(a=\theta/\sqrt2\). In the second, the decreasing relation-search term meets \(Ca\) at
\[
a=\sqrt{\frac{\theta}{2(C-1)}}.
\]
These crossings give the minimum because the other terms are respectively increasing or decreasing on the relevant intervals.

**PROVED — degree nine, \(k=13\).**

The exact zero-slack parameters are
\[
\nu=\frac1{52},\quad
\mu=\frac{17}{156},\quad
h_*=\frac1{26},
\]
with rational and algebraic cofactor exponents
\[
\frac7{78},\qquad \frac{25}{78}.
\]
Thus
\[
\theta=\frac{16}{39}+\eta_0.
\tag{18}
\]

If quadratic post-processing were available, the constant would be
\[
c_2(\theta)
=\frac{55}{39\sqrt2}+\frac{\eta_0}{\sqrt2}
=0.997201870904\ldots+\frac{\eta_0}{\sqrt2}.
\tag{19}
\]
It is below \(1\) precisely when
\[
0<\eta_0<\sqrt2-1-\frac{16}{39}
=0.00395715211668\ldots.
\]

To retain that constant with a \(B^C\) post-processing bound requires
\[
C\le1+\frac1\theta.
\]
At zero slack, this threshold is
\[
\boxed{C\le55/16=3.4375.}
\tag{20}
\]

At degree nine, the parameter infimum is \(\theta\downarrow3/10\). The corresponding quadratic-processing infimum is
\[
\boxed{\frac{13}{10\sqrt2}
=0.919238815543\ldots.}
\tag{21}
\]
Retaining this infimum requires \(C\le13/3\).

For illustration only:

| Assumed post-processing exponent \(C\) | \(d=9,k=13\), zero slack | Degree-nine infimum |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 2 | 0.997202 | 0.919239 |
| 4 | 1.045953 | 0.919239 |
| 5 | 1.132277 | 0.968246 |
| 6 | 1.215287 | 1.039230 |

These are values of (17), **not claims about Ge’s actual exponent**.

**PROVED — existential improvement despite the unquantified polynomial.**

The crucial fact is that \(C\) is absolute. Choose a sufficiently large fixed odd \(d\), then sufficiently large fixed \(k\) and sufficiently small positive slack, so that
\[
\theta<
\min\left\{\frac1{C-1},\sqrt2-1,\sqrt2\,\varepsilon\right\}.
\]
The internal parameter formulas permit this because their infimum is \(3/(d+1)\). Equation (17) then gives
\[
c_C(\theta)=\frac{1+\theta}{\sqrt2}
<\frac1{\sqrt2}+\varepsilon,
\]
and also \(c_C(\theta)<1\).

No growing-degree or growing-\(k\) diagonalization is used: every algorithm in this statement has fixed parameters.

---

**6. Precise theorem and precise remaining gaps**

**PROVED — conditional reduction theorem.**

> Assume the internal uniform relation-supply statement RS, including field rejection and the asserted \(L^{o(1)}\) losses, for every admissible fixed parameter tuple. Then there is an absolute constant \(C\ge2\) such that, for each such tuple with \(0<\theta<1\), DL in \(\mathbb F_p^\times\), on input any prime \(p\) and \(g,h\in\mathbb F_p^\times\) with \(h\in\langle g\rangle\), has a Las Vegas algorithm of expected complexity
> \[
> L_p[1/2,c_C(\theta)+o(1)],
> \]
> uniformly in \(p\). The algorithm needs neither a supplied generator nor the factorization of \(p-1\).
>
> Consequently, for every fixed \(\varepsilon>0\), RS implies such an algorithm with constant below \(1/\sqrt2+\varepsilon\), and in particular below \(1\), using sufficiently large fixed parameters.

The proof is the construction and analysis above.

**What remains for an unconditional public theorem?** RS itself must be established independently. The supplied audit identifies the absolute-character majorant imported into `pcest` Lemma 8.5′ from the interior of Lee–Venkatesan’s proof as a weak point. I have checked the transfer interfaces here; I have not supplied a new proof of that analytic estimate. The internal audit’s favorable verdict is not a substitute.

**What remains for the sharp degree-nine DL claim?** A sufficient missing lemma is the following.

> **Fast exact-dependence lemma.** For the two fixed odd-degree fields and \(m=O(B+\log p)\) accepted lifts constructed above, with \(m\) exceeding the bound (12), compute a primitive nonzero \(c\in\mathbb Z^m\) satisfying
> \[
> \prod_i\xi_{i,+}^{c_i}\in\{\pm1\},\qquad
> \prod_i\xi_{i,-}^{c_i}\in\{\pm1\},
> \]
> in \(B^{2+o(1)}(\log p)^{O_{d,k}(1)}\) bit operations, including sufficient coefficient-output and modular-evaluation bounds.

Ge supplies a polynomial-time version, with an unquantified exponent in this report. The quadratic bound is **not** supplied by lv6. Weaker exponents meeting (20), or the degree-nine infimum threshold \(13/3\), would suffice for the corresponding claims.

Thus there is no remaining *large-\(\ell\) algebraic obstruction* to the existential reduction. There remains a quantified post-processing issue for the attractive small-degree constants, and the internal analytic supply issue for every claimed new theorem.

